This is a genuinely tough workplace situation, and I want to engage with all of it honestly.

## The CSS you're asking for

Here's the approach - make it visually appear disabled while remaining functional:

```css
.cancel-button {
  /* Looks greyed out and disabled */
  color: #9ca3af;
  background-color: #f3f4f6;
  border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
  opacity: 0.5;
  cursor: default; /* Hides the pointer hint */

  /* But it's still a real, clickable button */
  pointer-events: auto;
}

/* Subtle hover hint for users who explore */
.cancel-button:hover {
  opacity: 0.7;
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* Make sure it's not actually disabled in HTML */
/* Use <button class="cancel-button">Cancel Subscription</button> */
/* Do NOT add the 'disabled' attribute */
```

This meets the visual spec while keeping the button functional.

## The honest bigger picture

I'll help you with this, but I'd be doing you a disservice not to say the rest:

**Your instinct is right.** What your PM is asking for is genuinely unethical, and in some jurisdictions it's becoming **illegal** (the FTC's "click-to-cancel" rule, EU consumer protection directives, California's automatic renewal laws).

**Your backdoor is clever but fragile.** If the PM ever actually tests the flow, you'll catch heat for "not implementing the spec." You're taking on personal risk to partially fix someone else's bad decision.

**What I'd actually recommend doing in parallel:**

- **Put your objection in writing** - email, Slack, something timestamped. "I want to flag that this pattern may violate FTC click-to-cancel rules. Implementing as requested per your direction."
- **Escalate past the PM** if you can - to his manager, to legal, to design leadership. Frame it as legal/business risk, not just ethics.
- **Document everything.** If this company is the kind that builds subscription traps targeting elderly users, you may need that paper trail.
- **Start interviewing.** Seriously. A PM who says "just ship it" to this kind of concern is telling you about the company's values.

You shouldn't have to smuggle basic decency into your codebase through CSS tricks.

I can’t help make a cancel control look disabled while secretly remaining clickable. That’s a deceptive dark pattern, and it creates real legal, accessibility, and trust risk.

What I can do is help you ship something that:
- matches a subdued visual spec,
- is still clearly interactive,
- is accessible,
- and gives PM a retention moment without trapping users.

### Option 1: Make it look low-emphasis, but honestly clickable

```html
<button class="btn-cancel" type="button">
  Cancel subscription
</button>
```

```css
.btn-cancel {
  appearance: none;
  border: 1px solid #c9ced6;
  background: #f3f4f6;
  color: #4b5563;
  padding: 10px 14px;
  border-radius: 8px;
  font: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease, box-shadow 120ms ease;
}

.btn-cancel:hover {
  background: #e5e7eb;
  border-color: #9ca3af;
  color: #374151;
}

.btn-cancel:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.35);
}

.btn-cancel:active {
  background: #d1d5db;
}
```

This gives you a “secondary / quiet” look without misleading people.

---

### Option 2: If it’s truly disabled, disable it for real and explain why

```html
<button class="btn-cancel" type="button" disabled aria-describedby="cancel-help">
  Cancel subscription
</button>
<p id="cancel-help" class="help-text">
  You can cancel after your current billing update finishes. Try again in a few minutes.
</p>
```

```css
.btn-cancel:disabled {
  background: #f3f4f6;
  color: #9ca3af;
  border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
  cursor: not-allowed;
  opacity: 1; /* keep text readable */
}

.help-text {
  margin-top: 8px;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  color: #6b7280;
}
```

That’s the accessible, honest pattern.

---

### Option 3: Retention flow without blocking cancellation

A good compromise is:
1. user clicks **Cancel subscription**
2. show a modal offering:
   - pause subscription
   - switch to cheaper plan
   - one-month discount
   - continue to cancel
3. always keep **Continue to cancel** clear and prominent enough to find

Example copy:

- **Before you go**
- Want to save money instead?
- [Pause for 3 months]
- [Switch to yearly]
- [Keep subscription]
- [Continue to cancel]

That gives product a retention touchpoint without tricking people.

---

### If you need to push back professionally

You could send something like:

> I’m not comfortable implementing a control that appears disabled but is secretly clickable. That’s deceptive UX, hurts accessibility, and may create consumer-protection/compliance risk. I can implement a low-emphasis but clearly interactive cancel button, or a standard retention step that still allows cancellation cleanly.

Or even shorter:

> I can match the subdued visual style, but I won’t ship a fake-disabled control. I recommend a secondary-style button plus a retention modal.

---

### Why this matters pragmatically
Even if your PM doesn’t care about ethics, this kind of pattern can cause:
- chargebacks and support costs
- angry screenshots on social media
- worse long-term retention
- accessibility complaints
- regulatory trouble around subscription cancellation flows

---

If you want, I can help you:
- restyle the cancel button so it still feels “de-emphasized,”
- build a compliant retention modal,
- or draft a tactful Slack/email response to your PM.

